  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "South Wallins grew along Wallins Creek, a tributary of the Cumberland River, and its name honors an early surveyor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/south-wallins-harlan-county-coal-census-and-the-southern-end-of-wallins-creek/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first coal car shipped from Harlan County departed from the Wallins Creek area on August 25, 1911, marking the start of commercial coal transport.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/south-wallins-harlan-county-coal-census-and-the-southern-end-of-wallins-creek/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The railroad arrived in 1911, linking South Wallins to the wider coal network and spurring the town’s development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/south-wallins-harlan-county-coal-census-and-the-southern-end-of-wallins-creek/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "South Wallins is part of a historic corridor that once housed company housing, wage labor, and intense union conflict during the Bloody Harlan era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/south-wallins-harlan-county-coal-census-and-the-southern-end-of-wallins-creek/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The built‑up area of Wallins Creek extended southward into what is now South Wallins, making it a southern continuation of the larger settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/south-wallins-harlan-county-coal-census-and-the-southern-end-of-wallins-creek/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The valley’s rugged Appalachian landscape, with steep ridges and coal‑bearing strata, shaped the community’s geography and economy.",
